<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aragón (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aragón is a river of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> that rises in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> near the Somport pass, flows past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaca">Jaca</a> and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. Its upper valley was the home of the Iacetani in antiquity, and in the 9th century it gave its name to the County of Aragon, from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a> grew.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aragon-river.jpg" alt="Aragón (river)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Jsanchezes at Spanish Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valle_rio_aragon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
